# Pongal Greetings, 1963

I offer my greetings! Though poverty, longing, and sorrow assail us, on the new day of Pongal alone - a festival we have accepted as our own - we bathe in fresh water, wear new clothes, and celebrate with overflowing joy. Why? Because we deeply desire that the virtue of honouring labour should spread throughout the world.

Why did people praise the wealth-giving sun? Because it nourishes living beings. Why honour the greatness of farmers? Because those who give food give life. Why did we honour cattle? Because they lovingly give sweet milk. This festival is therefore a noble festival of gratitude.

Our Valluvar declared: “There may be redemption even for one who destroys every other good; there is none for one who destroys a good deed done to him.” The way of the Kural must spread across the world, and we must demonstrate it through our conduct.

The Kural did not tell us to retreat into a dark cave or forest. To gain excellence it said: “Learn flawlessly what must be learned; having learned it, live worthy of it.” Let us study the lesson shown by forest, cultivated field, lake, and bull. Let us understand the farming principle that weeds must be removed when they sprout.

Understanding this, let us accept what is good, reject what is bad, and labour every day so our noble country reaches a condition admired by the world. Festivals are one way to relieve the weariness caused by work; their joy and abundance exist so that labour may gain honour.

The Rising Sun does not say, “Yesterday I gave perfect light; today I shall rest.” Like it, let us continue labouring so the world may prosper. With a full heart, I greet the noble people who perform such excellent work.

Your elder brother, Annadurai

---NOTES---
The Tamil source is preserved exactly, including irregular spacing. The quoted couplet is Tirukkural 110, and the later quotation adapts Tirukkural 391.

